Introduction
The advent of Large Language Models (LLMs), like GPT-4<|CIT-MASK|> has marked a significant milestone in the field of natural language processing (NLP). These models have not only excelled in various NLP tasks but have also found widespread application in user-interactive settings such as chatbots and virtual assistants. This application involves an extremely high number of users, up to hundreds of millions daily. To serve in real-time at this scale, a low-latency system is not only cost-saving but also crucial for keeping the service running. In addition, the sheer scale of the service means that even a slight improvement in the latency of LLMs can greatly contribute to both the provider and the community. Consequently, optimizing LLM latency has become a crucial and impactful research focus.

Unfortunately, LLMs currently suffer from high latency, especially in long-form generation, as autoregressive LLMs generate tokens one by one.
An emerging solution, known as speculative decoding<|CIT-MASK|>, shows potential to mitigate this issue.
In speculative decoding, a draft model (which is smaller and faster) generates $k$ tokens in each step (with $k$ being a hyperparameter),
and these tokens are then reviewed by a target model (which is larger and slower) in parallel.
The target model will accept any tokens aligned with its output and further generate one token; the process happens all in a single run while maintaining the output distribution identical to the autoregressive one by the target model itself.
As a larger number of expected accepted tokens per run indicates fewer target model runs, the efficiency of speculative decoding can be attributed to two main factors: the expected number of tokens accepted by the target model per run, and the latency of drafting.

\begin{figure}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure/acceptance_rate_GSM8k.pdf}
\vspace{-1em}
\caption{The probability of acceptance of draft tokens in relation to their positions in a single step of speculative decoding, evaluated on FLAN-T5-\textsc{small}, \textsc{base}, and \textsc{large} models. The draft model generates 30 tokens at each step.}
\vspace{-3mm}
\label{fig:acc_rate}
\end{figure}

\begin{figure*}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figure/model_fig.pdf}
\vspace{-1em}
\caption{Cascade Speculative Sampling uses LLMs of multiple sizes to generate and verify tokens. The model at the inner layer $\mathcal{M}_{d(i)}$ generates tokens proposed to and verified by LLMs at the next outer layer, $\mathcal{M}_{d(i+1)}$. This recursive process forms the vertical cascade.
After a vertical cascade, a smaller $\mathcal{M}_{d(i)}$ will serve as the target model and perform a vertical cascade. The process repeats until the smaller model becomes a statistical language model that will generate autoregressively forming the horizontal cascade.
}
\label{fig:the_main_fig}
\end{figure*}

Herein lies a trade-off between these two factors: to increase the expected number of accepted tokens, we can use a larger draft model or generate more draft tokens per target run. However, both of these approaches will significantly increase the latency of drafting.
Consequently, the maximum speed improvement achievable by the current speculative decoding algorithm depends on finding an optimal balance between model size and the number of generated tokens. Previous work by<|CIT-MASK|> has shown that very small models (e.g., less than 1/100$th$ the size of the target model), with varying values of $k$, are optimal for most tasks they experimented with, suggesting that the cost of drafting can be a limiting factor in the speculative decoding algorithm. This underscores that improving drafting efficiency is crucial for further enhancing the performance of speculative decoding.

In light of this, there exist a type of draft models, including statistical language models like bigram language models, that are immune to drafting efficiency issues. Owing to their simple structure, these models incur negligible latency and computational resource costs compared to neural language models, thereby avoiding the aforementioned trade-off. Therefore, assuming enough parallel computing power to perform computation on additional $k$ tokens with similar time cost<|CIT-MASK|>, utilizing speculative decoding with a statistical language model as the draft model can generally and intuitively enhance the efficiency of a neural language model.
However, due to their limited capabilities, speculative decoding with statistical language models alone may not yield optimal results compared to using a well-sized neural language model from the same family as the target model when the neural language model is available.

Based on the above analysis, we propose \textbf{Cascade Speculative Drafting (CS Drafting)}, a speculative execution algorithm that comprises multiple draft models, starting with the smallest being a statistical language model. Each neural draft model reviews generations from a smaller model and then proposes its reviewed content to either a larger draft model or the target model. We refer to this tiered speculative decoding approach as the \textit{Vertical Cascade}.

Furthermore, our analysis in Figure \ref{fig:acc_rate} reveals an interesting pattern in the expected acceptance rate during the drafting step: tokens generated later in the sequence by the draft model show a progressively lower probability of being accepted by the target model\footnote{A theoretical analysis is provided in Section~\ref{sec:theoretical_analysis}. Note that this probability differs from the acceptance rate, which is the probability of a token being accepted conditioned on the previous tokens' acceptance.}. This indicates that later tokens from draft models, more prone to rejection, contribute less to the expected number of accepted tokens, yet incur the same latency. Based on this observation and our practice, we suggest the use of smaller, faster draft models for generating these high-rejection tokens, forming the \textit{Horizontal Cascade}. Along with the aforementioned \textit{Vertical Cascade}, these strategies compose our complete CS Drafting approach, as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:the_main_fig}.

Through theoretical analysis and empirical studies,
we demonstrate that the CS Drafting algorithm outperforms speculative decoding in terms of latency across various tasks and settings, achieving an additional speedup of up to 72\% over speculative decoding. These findings highlight the practical advantages and efficiency enhancements offered by both vertical and horizontal cascades.

Our main contributions are summarized as follows:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*, nolistsep]
\setlength{\itemsep}{1mm}
\item We introduce Cascade Speculative Drafting (CS Drafting), a speculative-execution-based algorithm that improves language model inference speed without sacrificing generation quality.
\item We provide theoretical analyses supporting the effectiveness of the proposed CS Drafting approach.
\item
We conduct empirical experiments showing that CS Drafting achieves further speedup over speculative decoding across different tasks and settings.
\end{itemize}
Related Work
\subsection{Efficienct Methods for Language Model Inference}
In the era of large language models, the efficiency during inference becomes a key to model service. To reduce the model inference cost and speed up, several efficient methods have been proposed, including pruning, knowledge distillation and quantization<|CIT-MASK|>. Model pruning takes structured<|CIT-MASK|> or unstructured<|CIT-MASK|> methods to remove the redundant model parameters to reduce the storage memory and increase inference speed. Knowledge distillation takes the approach of transferring knowledge from a superior teacher model to a smaller student model<|CIT-MASK|>. 
Quantization maps high-precision data representations (e.g. 32 bits) into low-precision ones (e.g. 8 bits) to reduce memory consumption<|CIT-MASK|>.  

\subsection{Speculative Decoding}

 Recent works improved the speed of speculative decoding by experimenting with task-specific knowledge distillation<|CIT-MASK|>, or using approaches similar to speculative decoding to achieve a tradeoff between quality and speed

