Foundation models are transforming the field of natural language processing (NLP), demonstrating impressive zero-shot and few-shot generalization capabilities<|CIT-MASK|>. As a premier exemplar of such expansive language models, GPT-4 has radically altered our understanding of artificial intelligence, offering innovative solutions for almost all downstream NLP tasks by implementing foundational models in lieu of specialized, vertical ones<|CIT-MASK|>. This paradigm of pre-training and fine-tuning, though currently in vogue for NLP, has its roots in the realm of computer vision (CV), harking back to an era when tasks such as segmentation and object detection utilized pre-trained weights derived from ImageNet<|CIT-MASK|>. In the present trends of using foundation models, CV tasks tend to rely on visual-language pre-training models rather than purely visual models. For instance, the recent model CLIP<|CIT-MASK|> employs contrastive learning to train text and image encoders that establish a concordance between the two modalities, exhibiting prowess in diverse CV tasks, ranging from classification<|CIT-MASK|> and semantic segmentation<|CIT-MASK|> to image generation<|CIT-MASK|>. Notably, numerous tasks employ solely the image encoders from these visual-language models, signifying the extensive utility of a single image encoder's representational capacity across a myriad of CV tasks<|CIT-MASK|>.

